WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.334 --> 00:00:02.669
-Congrats on this cover.
You look gorgeous here.

00:00:02.669 --> 00:00:03.903
-Thank you. Thanks.
-Beautiful.

00:00:03.903 --> 00:00:06.606
[ Cheers and applause ]
-First time in Rio.

00:00:06.606 --> 00:00:08.307
-Oh, is it Rio?
-Yeah.

00:00:08.307 --> 00:00:10.310
-I almost said, "Where is it?"
-Yeah. Brazil, Rio.

00:00:10.310 --> 00:00:12.878
-Did you have fun there?
-First time. I loved it so much.

00:00:12.878 --> 00:00:14.748
It's a beach town,
so I feel like, whenever I can,

00:00:14.748 --> 00:00:17.150
like, look out the window and
watch beach volleyball going on,

00:00:17.150 --> 00:00:18.718
I'm happy.
-You love that, yeah.

00:00:18.718 --> 00:00:20.353
-Did you do any escape rooms
in there?

00:00:20.353 --> 00:00:24.023
-No, I do escape rooms
in a lot of towns that I go to.

00:00:24.023 --> 00:00:25.692
-I see that.
-But not Rio. I didn't check.

00:00:25.692 --> 00:00:27.193
-You didn't do that.
-I love escape rooms.

00:00:27.193 --> 00:00:28.160
-Where else have you done
escape rooms?

00:00:28.160 --> 00:00:30.196
-I've done one here.

00:00:30.196 --> 00:00:32.231
I've done one in L.A.,
Santa Barbara.

00:00:32.231 --> 00:00:35.368
-Are you good at these rooms?
-I love them.

00:00:35.368 --> 00:00:37.370
I studied criminal psychology
in college.

00:00:37.370 --> 00:00:38.938
I don't know
if I've told you that.

00:00:38.938 --> 00:00:41.708
So, I'm really into, like,
all of that kind of --

00:00:41.708 --> 00:00:45.244
kind of detective work
and mystery-solving.

00:00:45.244 --> 00:00:46.746
All that kind of stuff.
I love it.

00:00:46.746 --> 00:00:48.514
-You go in a room,
you have to solve a puzzle

00:00:48.514 --> 00:00:49.715
to get to the next room, right?
-And it's, like, a great way

00:00:49.715 --> 00:00:52.084
to, like, get, you know,
new friends in the group

00:00:52.084 --> 00:00:54.654
or, like, whatever, just to go
do something together and fun.

00:00:54.654 --> 00:00:57.156
-Are you fun when you do this?
Or are you intense?

00:00:57.156 --> 00:00:59.692
Would you yell at me
if I was in a room with you?

00:00:59.692 --> 00:01:01.494
-I'm, like -- I'm like,
super nice,

00:01:01.494 --> 00:01:03.029
if you're, like, there
to win with me.

00:01:03.029 --> 00:01:04.430
You know what I mean?
But, like, if you're,

00:01:04.430 --> 00:01:07.933
like, goofing off,
no, I'm not nice about it.

00:01:07.933 --> 00:01:10.503
-I don't know if
I would goof off so much.

00:01:10.503 --> 00:01:12.171
Maybe, like, going in,
I would goof a little.

00:01:12.171 --> 00:01:13.405
-No, we'll work as a team.
We'd be good.

00:01:13.405 --> 00:01:14.907
We should go together one time.
-We should, yeah.

00:01:14.907 --> 00:01:18.144
But what kind of things do you
see when you go into the rooms?

00:01:18.144 --> 00:01:20.346
-Okay, so, like, every single
room is a different theme.

00:01:20.346 --> 00:01:22.948
So it could be a murder mystery.
It could be that you're, like --

00:01:22.948 --> 00:01:24.350
And you get a little story
before.

00:01:24.350 --> 00:01:25.784
So, you could be, like,
stuck in a spaceship,

00:01:25.784 --> 00:01:28.821
and you have an hour or 45
minutes to get out.

00:01:28.821 --> 00:01:32.224
Or you could be --
There was one where, like,

00:01:32.224 --> 00:01:34.760
your neighbor is a murderer
and you have to find out,

00:01:34.760 --> 00:01:37.530
like, who he murdered.
But you're stuck in one room,

00:01:37.530 --> 00:01:39.632
and all of the clues
are within the room.

00:01:39.632 --> 00:01:41.634
You're given no clues
before that,

00:01:41.634 --> 00:01:43.069
and you kind of just have to
figure a way out.

00:01:43.069 --> 00:01:44.537
-Wow, fun!
-But there's all different,

00:01:44.537 --> 00:01:45.938
like, themes.
You can them look up online

00:01:45.938 --> 00:01:48.040
and, like, see what you're into.
-Good.

00:01:48.040 --> 00:01:49.342
I would do that with you.
-Yeah.

00:01:49.342 --> 00:01:50.877
-Let's talk about what you're
doing with Reebok.

00:01:50.877 --> 00:01:52.545
Congrats on this thing.
-Thank you.

00:01:52.545 --> 00:01:54.880
-"Reebok by Gigi Hadid."

00:01:54.880 --> 00:01:58.150
-i just designed my first
collection for Reebok,

00:01:58.150 --> 00:02:01.320
which is my first activewear
athleisure collection.

00:02:01.320 --> 00:02:03.856
And I was a volleyball player
in high school.

00:02:03.856 --> 00:02:05.458
-I didn't know that.
-Yeah.

00:02:05.458 --> 00:02:07.193
And so, when I
moved to New York,

00:02:07.193 --> 00:02:11.330
I kind of always was in, like --
when I was going to college,

00:02:11.330 --> 00:02:14.533
I was always in workout clothes
and then trying to make those

00:02:14.533 --> 00:02:16.002
into, like, kind of
a fashion look

00:02:16.002 --> 00:02:17.270
so that I could go to school

00:02:17.270 --> 00:02:18.771
and then go straight to the gym
afterwards.

00:02:18.771 --> 00:02:21.908
And, like, that kind of became
this athleisure-style thing

00:02:21.908 --> 00:02:23.176
that people always ask me about.

00:02:23.176 --> 00:02:24.510
So when I did
my Reebok collection,

00:02:24.510 --> 00:02:26.012
I really wanted it
to be something

00:02:26.012 --> 00:02:28.314
where, if I was
a college student again,

00:02:28.314 --> 00:02:29.982
I feel like I could wear this
to school

00:02:29.982 --> 00:02:31.584
and feel, like,
great in it and cute.

00:02:31.584 --> 00:02:34.220
But then also it's something
that you can wear to the gym

00:02:34.220 --> 00:02:35.989
and work out, and
that's what it's made for.

00:02:35.989 --> 00:02:37.890
So, a lot of --
Right now, the streetwear,

00:02:37.890 --> 00:02:40.125
a lot of the time,
is not actually comfortable

00:02:40.125 --> 00:02:42.294
to work out in,
which I think is weird.

00:02:42.294 --> 00:02:44.797
-It just looks cool, yeah.
-Or, like, the dad shoes

00:02:44.797 --> 00:02:48.701
that cost like $1,000 to look
like your dad's old sneakers,

00:02:48.701 --> 00:02:50.936
and you, like, can't work out.
They're not good for your foot.

00:02:50.936 --> 00:02:52.538
So, I don't get that.
-Yeah, yeah.

00:02:52.538 --> 00:02:55.041
What the sense of that?
-So, yeah, this is style and --

00:02:55.041 --> 00:02:56.709
-But you did something different
when you were launching this.

00:02:56.709 --> 00:02:59.345
-Yeah, so, when I launched it,
my first idea was --

00:02:59.345 --> 00:03:01.547
you know, when we sat down
and we wanted to come up

00:03:01.547 --> 00:03:03.282
with a concept
for the collection,

00:03:03.282 --> 00:03:06.753
I wanted my high school coaches
to come to New York

00:03:06.753 --> 00:03:09.489
and do the press day with me,
because we have these press days

00:03:09.489 --> 00:03:11.590
that a lot of the time
feel very unpersonal

00:03:11.590 --> 00:03:13.692
and they're kind of, like,
strange to get through.

00:03:13.692 --> 00:03:15.895
And I thought,
"What would I want to do

00:03:15.895 --> 00:03:20.666
that I could be mostly myself
and be really inspired that day.

00:03:20.666 --> 00:03:22.935
So I asked Reebok to fly
my high school coaches out.

00:03:22.935 --> 00:03:25.571
We went to the
DREAM Charter School uptown

00:03:25.571 --> 00:03:27.239
and played with the girls'
volleyball team.

00:03:27.239 --> 00:03:30.709
And it was just so awesome and
a really nice way to launch it.

00:03:30.709 --> 00:03:32.044
It made me so happy.

00:03:32.044 --> 00:03:34.480
-This is Gigi with
the DREAM Charter School --

00:03:34.480 --> 00:03:37.750
East Harlem volleyball team.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:03:37.750 --> 00:03:42.655
And this is you playing.
-Yeah, there's me.

00:03:42.655 --> 00:03:46.325
-Were you good?
-I was pretty good, Jimmy.

00:03:46.325 --> 00:03:48.061
You would have probably gotten
scared of me on the court.

00:03:48.061 --> 00:03:49.696
Like, you know, guys would be
like, "Oh, yeah,

00:03:49.696 --> 00:03:51.096
let's go watch Gigi
on the volleyball court."

00:03:51.096 --> 00:03:52.798
By the end of the game,
they're like, "She's scary."

00:03:52.798 --> 00:03:55.434
Like, "I don't think she's cute
anymore" kind of vibe.

00:03:55.434 --> 00:03:57.336
-Really? You get right in there?
-Oh, yeah.

00:03:57.336 --> 00:04:00.439
And, like, my coach, we'd
have to have some calm-downs.

00:04:00.439 --> 00:04:02.442
Like, "You're not gonna be able
to start next game

00:04:02.442 --> 00:04:03.843
if I see you punch the ground
once."

00:04:03.843 --> 00:04:06.378
Like, I'd get mad and,
like, get really into it.

00:04:06.378 --> 00:04:10.316
But I played high school
volleyball so intensely.

00:04:10.316 --> 00:04:12.551
And club volleyball, I went to
Junior Olympic qualifiers.

00:04:12.551 --> 00:04:15.588
And it's just something
that I think is so important

00:04:15.588 --> 00:04:19.391
for young girls to
find that value in themselves

00:04:19.391 --> 00:04:21.994
in other things, and
things that inspire them

00:04:21.994 --> 00:04:23.496
or that they want to
get better at.

00:04:23.496 --> 00:04:26.165
And that, for me, was volleyball
and horseback riding and art.

00:04:26.165 --> 00:04:28.634
And I think
that having those things

00:04:28.634 --> 00:04:30.903
that you know
that you're great at

00:04:30.903 --> 00:04:32.871
and that you love
gives you the feeling

00:04:32.871 --> 00:04:34.707
that you can take those
and have a future with them.

00:04:34.707 --> 00:04:36.341
So it was really important
to me,

00:04:36.341 --> 00:04:39.379
at the DREAM Charter School,
to just show girls

00:04:39.379 --> 00:04:42.215
that, like, putting your mind
to something

00:04:42.215 --> 00:04:43.916
and really wanting
to get good at it

00:04:43.916 --> 00:04:46.118
can give you so much
of a bigger future.

00:04:46.118 --> 00:04:48.187
And I think that's great,
whatever it is you want to do.

00:04:48.187 --> 00:04:50.289
[ Cheers and applause ]
-Good for you.

00:04:50.289 --> 00:04:52.357
Congrats on that.
-Thank you.

00:04:52.357 --> 00:04:55.628
-Now, every time -- every time
you come on the show,

00:04:55.628 --> 00:04:58.697
we really end up getting in a
conversation about hamburgers.

00:04:58.697 --> 00:05:01.900
-Yeah. We love burgers.
-We love burgers. We really do.

00:05:01.900 --> 00:05:03.969
-And we think about each other
every time we eat burgers,

00:05:03.969 --> 00:05:05.971
'cause we're always trying
to one-up each other

00:05:05.971 --> 00:05:07.706
of who's going to find
the next best burger.

00:05:07.706 --> 00:05:09.241
-I found it.
-Okay, go!

00:05:09.241 --> 00:05:10.710
-I thought about you --
-Wait! Hold on.

00:05:10.710 --> 00:05:13.145
I have something for you, too.
You get the burgers that way,

00:05:13.145 --> 00:05:15.448
and I have something --
hold on -- over here

00:05:15.448 --> 00:05:17.817
that I need.
-What are you doing?

00:05:17.817 --> 00:05:20.119
-Okay, Jimmy --
-What are you getting?

00:05:20.119 --> 00:05:23.021
-I brought us something
to eat burgers with.

00:05:23.021 --> 00:05:24.924
Tiny hands.
[ Laughter ]

00:05:24.924 --> 00:05:27.025
"What?"

00:05:27.025 --> 00:05:28.727
-We're going to eat burgers
with tiny hands?

00:05:28.727 --> 00:05:30.730
-Yeah.
-Why?

00:05:30.730 --> 00:05:32.732
-'Cause I always bring you
something tiny.

00:05:32.732 --> 00:05:33.933
I brought you your tiny bag
last time.

00:05:33.933 --> 00:05:35.435
-Yeah, tiny bag.
I remember tiny bag.

00:05:35.435 --> 00:05:37.437
-And, look, we can model
with them, too.

00:05:37.437 --> 00:05:40.039
-It's actually not bad.
Yeah, okay.

00:05:40.039 --> 00:05:42.541
All right, so, wait -- this is
from a place called Upland.

00:05:42.541 --> 00:05:44.610
-Upland. Shout-out to Upland!
Thank you!

00:05:44.610 --> 00:05:47.380
-Upland. It's on --
where is it? -- 26th and Park.

00:05:47.380 --> 00:05:48.814
All right, get ready for this.
You're gonna freak out.

00:05:48.814 --> 00:05:50.149
It's avocado --
-You got to

00:05:50.149 --> 00:05:51.617
put your tiny hands on it.
-I know, I will. Sorry.

00:05:51.617 --> 00:05:53.085
[ Laughter ]
All right, here we go.

00:05:53.085 --> 00:05:54.653
Wait. It's hard
to hold the hamburger.

00:05:54.653 --> 00:05:56.155
It's hard to -- Wait.
How are you doing it?

00:05:56.155 --> 00:05:57.924
-Cheers. Like this.
-I know, but, gosh.

00:05:57.924 --> 00:05:59.324
You got really strong
fingers, man.

00:05:59.324 --> 00:06:00.860
-I'm a volleyball player.
Cheers.

00:06:00.860 --> 00:06:02.628
-All right, here we go. Cheers.
You're the best, Gigi.

00:06:02.628 --> 00:06:04.063
[ Light laughter ]

00:06:04.063 --> 00:06:06.832
[ Soft jazz plays ]

00:06:06.832 --> 00:06:08.601
-Wow!
-Right?

00:06:08.601 --> 00:06:10.002
-Mm-hmm.
-It's pretty good, right?

00:06:10.002 --> 00:06:12.704
-Avocado!
-Avocado in the house!

00:06:12.704 --> 00:06:14.941
Gigi Hadid, everybody!
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:06:14.941 --> 00:06:17.977
Her new line,
Reebok by Gigi Hadid,

00:06:17.977 --> 00:06:19.212
is available now.

